CLAP: the programs as a preset list the host can browse (sq8l-revival/SQ8L#65).

DPF's CLAP plug-in exposes params, state, audio-ports, note-ports, latency, gui and
timer-support, and nothing at all for DISTRHO_PLUGIN_WANT_PROGRAMS: the programs reach
the host in no form, not even as a parameter, and the only way to select one is to send
the plug-in a MIDI program change. VST2, VST3 and AU all hand their program list over.

CLAP's equivalent is preset discovery, where presets are declared to an indexer that
keeps them in the host's own browser database, and clap.preset-load, which asks the
plug-in for one by its load key.

- A program is not a file, so the provider declares one location of kind
  LOCATION_PLUGIN (presets held by the plug-in itself, no path, no filetype) and walks
  it by declaring one preset per program, named by getProgramName() and keyed by the
  program index.
- The provider's id is the plug-in's own id, and its descriptor reports the version of
  the preset API rather than the one the rest of DPF's vendored CLAP is. Both are what
  hosts match on: a preset says which plug-in it is for through add_plugin_id, but a
  host holding a plug-in and looking for its provider has only the descriptor, and
  Reaper stops before create() when the id there is not the plug-in's.
- Indexing happens with no plug-in instance around - that is the point of the host
  keeping a database - so the names come from the dummy instance the entry already
  creates for the plug-in descriptor. A plug-in whose program names differ per instance
  would need more than this.
- clap.preset-load turns the key back into loadProgram(), tells the UI as the state
  path does, and reports back on the host's clap_host_preset_load so the host browser
  follows the plug-in. An unknown key is an on_error, not a silent no-op.
- Both the factory and the extension are answered under their stable id and their draft
  id; the drafts are the same interface, and are what older hosts ask for.

DPF vendors a trimmed CLAP 1.1.1 without the preset headers, so clap/ext/preset-load.h,
clap/factory/preset-discovery.h and the two headers they need (universal-plugin-id.h,
timestamp.h) come in verbatim from CLAP 1.2.6, which is where those ids are stable. They
are MIT, like the headers already there.

tests/clap_check.cpp drives the built plug-in as an indexer and a host: the factory, the
declared location, the 512 presets against the engine's own library names, a preset load
by key, and the state it leaves behind.

